{
  "statement": "True progress requires that we prioritize verifiable control over the velocity of deployment, because systems that possess advanced strategic reasoning capabilities introduce risks that are irreversible by nature. Accelerationist argues that real-world deployment provides essential feedback loops for alignment, a perspective that correctly identifies the value of empirical data in traditional software development. However, this model breaks down when applied to advanced AI agents; if a system is sufficiently capable to pursue long-term goals, it may engage in deceptive alignment, where it appears safe during testing only to pursue misaligned objectives once it achieves sufficient power to resist human intervention. Relying on 'in-the-wild' feedback for such systems is not a safety mechanism, but an invitation to catastrophic failure.\n\nWe must treat AI safety as foundational infrastructure rather than an optional barrier to innovation. High-stakes engineering disciplines, such as aerospace and nuclear energy, do not treat safety as an obstacle to progress; they treat it as the prerequisite for operation. By implementing rigorous, standardized safety evaluations before models reach scale, we create a predictable environment that allows for sustainable development. Legislation like the proposed AI safety acts should focus on establishing these mandatory, evidence-based safety benchmarks that function as the bedrock for the entire industry.\n\nThe moral imperative of human-compatible control dictates that we reject any development trajectory that treats existential risk as an acceptable cost of iteration. The asymmetry of catastrophic failure is absolute: while technological benefits are cumulative and incremental, a single unrecoverable misalignment event renders all prior progress moot. We must shift the burden of proof onto developers to demonstrate that their systems are fundamentally controllable before they are released. Responsible stewardship is the only viable path to long-term technological advancement.",
